Starting at `1`, keep doubling until the value reaches `n` or becomes larger than it. If the loop lands exactly on `n`, the number is a power of two.

Dry run for `n = 16`:

`1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 8 -> 16`, so the method returns `true`. For `n = 18`, the next value after `16` is `32`, which is already too large, so the answer is `false`.

```go
func isPowerOfTwo(n int) bool {
    if n <= 0 {
        return false
    }

    value := 1
    for value < n {
        value *= 2
    }

    return value == n
}
```

The edge cases `1`, `0`, and negative numbers are handled directly. The variable stays within the normal positive integer range for valid platform inputs.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`

Space complexity: `O(1)`

The recursive rule is small: `1` is a power of two, an odd number greater than `1` is not, and an even number can be checked again after dividing it by `2`.

Dry run for `n = 8`:

`8 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1`, so the method returns `true`. For `n = 6`, the value is even once, then `3` is odd and the result is `false`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfTwo(int n) {
        if (n == 1) {
            return true;
        }

        if (n <= 0 || n % 2 != 0) {
            return false;
        }

        return isPowerOfTwo(n / 2);
    }
}
```

The input is not changed. The recursion stops at `1`, and values like `0` or negative numbers are rejected.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`

Space complexity: `O(log n)` because of the recursive calls.

A power of two has an integer base-2 logarithm. For example, `log2(32)` is `5`, while `log2(10)` is not an integer.

The bitwise check is kept for the final test, so the code avoids comparing decimal floating point values directly. It also checks that the input is positive.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isPowerOfTwo(n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        const exponent = Math.log2(n);
        return Number.isInteger(exponent);
    }
}
```

This is short, but it depends on JavaScript's numeric and logarithm functions. For the integer inputs used by the platform, it gives the expected result.

Time complexity: `O(1)`

Space complexity: `O(1)`

A power of two has only one `1` bit in its binary form. For example, `8` is `1000`. If we subtract 1, it becomes `0111`, so `n & (n - 1)` becomes zero.

The positive check is important because `0` would also pass the bit expression. This code does not change any input.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfTwo(int n) {
        return n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(1)`

Space complexity: `O(1)`
